  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nMorning water vapor imagery shows an upper level low moving into\nnorthern Nevada and a trough extending south into Southern\nCalifornia. While the main band of precipitation has moved out,\nresidual showers continue over the coastal waters and into San\nDiego County. Expect these showers to come to an end by mid-\nmorning as the trough departs and drier air filters in. The\nremainder of the day will be cool and dry with highs in the 60s\nacross the coastal basin, 40s in the mountains, mid 50s in the\nhigh desert, and low 70s in the low desert.\n\nA short wave on the back side of the low, currently along the\ncoast near the OR/CA border, will rotate down through interior CA\nlater today, passing by to our north and east. Surface high\npressure building over the Great Basin behind this wave will drive\nvery weak offshore flow here on Saturday morning. This will lead \nto slightly warmer and drier weather. Onshore flow returns on\nSunday with little change in temperatures. Overall fair weather\nthis weekend with some high clouds at times.\n\nAnother upper level trough quickly moves through California Sunday\nnight into Monday morning. Several members of each of the global\nensembles remain dry with this trough (about 26% of the total\nensemble space). Most of the members have at least light\nprecipitation, so have a slight chance for the mountains westward\nSunday night into Monday morning, though any accumulation will\nlikely be limited to a few hundredths. Snow levels will be near\n6500 ft, but given the lack of moisture expect only a few\nflurries. Upper level ridging moves over late Monday and Tuesday\nfor drier and warmer weather with another brief round of weak\noffshore flow Tuesday morning. \n\nDifferences in the upper level pattern really begin to emerge as\nwe head into Wednesday. The deterministic GFS still becomes highly\namplified, bringing a deep trough into So Cal (and another deep\nclosed low over the Southeast US) by late Wednesday. This\nsolution, which would result in much colder and wetter weather,\nremains an outlier and isn't supported by the EC/Canadian\nensembles or even most of the GEFS ensemble members. Most of the\nsolutions point to more of a zonal flow pattern with the\nmoisture/precipitation aimed at Northern California. For now the\nofficial forecast has a 15-20% chance of precipitation on \nWednesday. Once this trough passes, upper level high pressure\nbegins to build in from the west. Weak offshore flow could develop\nas early as Thanksgiving morning, strengthening into Friday. For\nThanksgiving Day, high temperatures are forecast to be anywhere\nfrom 2 to 7 degrees above normal, warming to around 5 to 10\ndegrees above normal by Friday.",
